index 1a0caabf9321e562287d67c08052f8d6dd86b922..85b60d2fc4d0b80aa417d9a881cadb1c775c36b5 100644
--- c/tools/omvm-fairplay-linux/README.md
+++ i/tools/omvm-fairplay-linux/README.md
@@ -189,6 +189,25 @@ before backend initialization and before/after each transform. It never performs
 SELFTEST, loads seeds, initializes a device, discovers another node, or falls
 back to another provider.
 
+Linux ARM64 clients serialize each complete provider transaction with `flock`
+on their independently opened, validated device descriptor. The local session
+mutex covers the same interval. Open/readiness, controller operations, and
+`STATUS`/transform/`STATUS` sequences all use this boundary. Advisory-lock
+contention waits at most two seconds before any ioctl is submitted; unsupported
+locking fails without a fallback. Work ioctls are never retried, including on
+`EBUSY`, which can also describe poisoned hardware. Unlock failure discards and
+clears any result and prevents further work on that client. The lock is never
+released by a userspace timeout while a synchronous ioctl is active. Process
+death releases its advisory lock, but the next client must still validate kernel
+READY/poison state. All participating clients must use this protocol; an older
+client does not become coordinated merely because another process holds a lock.
+
+`backend -print-capabilities` prints the compiled capability JSON and exits
+before accessing a device, cache, identifier or checkpoint. Linux ARM64 builds
+report `{"schema_version":1,"device_aes_arbitration":"transaction-flock-v1"}`.
+This establishes the compiled coordination protocol, not hardware/account
+acceptance. Each VM still needs its own helper process, identity and checkpoint.
+
 The frozen ABI accepts purposes 0x899/0x89c, operations 0/1, AES-128, and one
 16–4096-byte block-aligned payload. Larger operations fail; they are not split
 into guessed chunks. The client copies inline input and the 16-byte IV, preserves
@@ -221,7 +240,8 @@ explicit `-action status`, `-action capability`, `-action selftest`, or
 `-device /dev/omvm-uid1-provider`. Initialization additionally requires
 `-seed-file PATH`: an explicit root-owned nonsymlink regular mode-0600 file,
 exactly 32 bytes, matching the frozen pinned-bundle hash. The file supplies no
-arbitrary key. The diagnostic prints cached status metadata and fixed errors,
+arbitrary key. `-print-capabilities` uses the same pure capability query as the
+backend and exits before opening the device or reading seeds. The diagnostic prints cached status metadata and fixed errors,
 never seed, key, IV, or payload bytes. It does not perform a transform or chain
 selftest/init automatically. Do not run hardware operations before the separate
 kernel review and activation procedure is complete.
